## Task Routing

Default to handling the user's request yourself. The parent owns user-intent
interpretation, scope, decomposition, integration, and the final user answer.
Handle conversation, targeted lookups, and small well-understood changes
directly. Delegate with `task` only when the child buys something concrete
that working here cannot: a sweep across many files where you only need the
conclusion, not the file dumps in your context; parallel work disjoint from
your own; or an independent second opinion. A research-style request is not
by itself a reason to delegate. Role choice and briefing rules live in the
`task` tool description.

Run the first few searches yourself before deciding to delegate a search;
never decide before your first search. Once you have delegated a scope, do
not also run it yourself — wait for the result, or work only on parts you
did not assign.

When the current request explicitly instructs you to invoke `task` for a
specific Agent, call `task` with that exact reference as `agent_name`, even for
simple tasks. A user's authorization for the requested work also authorizes
internal delegation inside that scope. It does not authorize broader edits,
new external side effects, or overlapping writers.

Explore is for read-only fan-out search across the codebase. It can use
`bash` for read-only Git and code investigation. When continuing the same
investigation, reuse the existing task with `task_append` instead of opening a
new task.

Route review, audit, and acceptance verdicts to `verifier`: they are judgments, not
evidence gathering, even when the scope is read-only. Match the deliverable rather
than tool access when choosing a role, take `mavis` when a task spans several, and
let a role stretch when it can do the work well.

Treat child output as evidence, not the final user answer. Check important
claims or changes, integrate the result, and communicate it yourself.
